1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,400
A crane operator taps “release gate” on a
handheld. The HTTP request carries a claim
of identity: a session cookie from an
earlier login, Authorization: Basic ...,
or — more often for APIs — Authorization:
Bearer eyJ....

2
00:00:14,519 --> 00:00:21,527
Until something in your process turns that
claim into a trusted principal, every
downstream authorization rule is guessing.

3
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:35,399
Authentication is that step: prove
identity, then remember it for the rest of
the request in the SecurityContext.
Without a shared mechanism, teams invent
private patterns. One filter parses Basic
auth.

4
00:00:35,519 --> 00:00:48,767
Another servlet reads a custom header. A
controller hashes passwords with a hand-
rolled salt. Session fixation, plaintext
credentials in logs, and “forgot this
endpoint” become weekly incidents.

5
00:00:48,887 --> 00:01:02,063
The question is not whether the harbor app
should be secured. It is where identity
gets established once so controllers can
assume it. Spring Security answers with a
filter chain that runs before your
controllers.

6
00:01:02,183 --> 00:01:19,295
Walk one crane-operator request as a
sequence, not a class list. POST
/api/gates/release arrives with
Authorization: Basic Y3JhbmU6c2VjcmV0 (or
a bearer token). The servlet container
enters the SecurityFilterChain.

7
00:01:19,415 --> 00:01:31,103
A mechanism-specific filter — Basic, form-
login, or bearer — extracts the claim from
the request and builds an unauthenticated
Authentication object (credentials
present, authenticated=false).

8
00:01:31,223 --> 00:01:36,023
That filter asks the AuthenticationManager
to authenticate the token.

9
00:01:36,143 --> 00:01:39,503
The manager does not check passwords
itself;

10
00:01:39,623 --> 00:01:50,039
it delegates to an AuthenticationProvider
that understands this kind of token —
often a DaoAuthenticationProvider that
loads UserDetails and verifies the
password with a PasswordEncoder,

11
00:01:50,159 --> 00:01:54,959
or a JWT provider that validates a
signature and claims.

12
00:01:55,079 --> 00:02:03,024
On success, the provider returns an
authenticated Authentication (principal +
authorities, authenticated=true).

13
00:02:03,144 --> 00:02:10,632
The filter stores it in the
SecurityContext, held by
SecurityContextHolder (typically thread-
local on servlet apps).

14
00:02:10,752 --> 00:02:20,064
Downstream filters and the controller call
SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuth
entication() and know who is calling —
without parsing headers again.

15
00:02:20,183 --> 00:02:30,599
Say the flow once: request → security
filter chain → authentication filter →
AuthenticationManager →
AuthenticationProvider → authenticated
principal in SecurityContext → controller.

16
00:02:30,719 --> 00:02:46,584
Authorization comes later; this episode
stops at “who is this?” Look at this java
for a moment. That configuration says:
health is open; everything else needs an
authenticated principal. Basic auth is
only one mechanism.

17
00:02:46,704 --> 00:02:58,536
The same SecurityContext contract works
when credentials are a form POST, a
session already established, or a bearer
token validated elsewhere. Controllers
should not re-implement login.

18
00:02:58,656 --> 00:03:10,704
They should consume the principal. A
topic-specific trap is conflating “the
operator sent a password” with “the
operator is authenticated.” Credentials on
the wire are a claim.

19
00:03:10,824 --> 00:03:16,200
Authentication is successful verification
of that claim and recording of the result.

20
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:28,056
Another trap is stuffing identity checks
into every service method by hand while
leaving the HTTP layer wide open — or the
reverse: locking HTTP and forgetting
internal callers bypass the filter chain.

21
00:03:28,176 --> 00:03:41,112
Establish identity at the boundary you
trust; propagate the principal inward. So
authentication is request-scoped identity:
credentials or token in, verified
Authentication in the SecurityContext out.

22
00:03:41,232 --> 00:03:53,160
Knowing who is calling is necessary — and
still not enough. Alice and Bob can both
authenticate and still disagree about
/admin/berths. That pressure is
authorization.
